window.PhoenixSiteSearchIndex={"generated_at_utc":"2026-11-03T23:17:58.093910Z","count":13,"pages":[{"title":"Antibiotics and viral illness","url":"/antibiotics-and-viral-illness/","description":"Antibiotics cannot cure colds, influenza or other viral infections because they are designed to disrupt bacterial structures and processes that viruses do...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Antibiotics and viral illness Antibiotics cannot cure colds, influenza or other viral infections because they are designed to disrupt bacterial structures and processes that viruses do... Antibiotics cannot cure colds, influenza or other viral infections because they are designed to disrupt bacterial structures and processes that viruses do not possess. Taking one for an uncomplicated viral illness will not kill the virus or reliably shorten the illness. Antibiotics and viral illness 6d8e45 Updated 2026 11 03 Introduction The practical rule is not “never use antibiotics for respiratory symptoms”. Viral infections can resemble bacterial illnesses, and a viral infection may occasionally be followed by a bacterial complication. The right question is whether there is evidence of a susceptible bacterial infection that is likely to benefit from treatment. A clinician may use the symptom pattern, examination, risk factors and sometimes a test to decide. If an antibiotic is prescribed, take it exactly as directed; do not borrow one, share it, save leftovers or alter the course without professional advice. The shortest decision path A cough, sore throat, blocked nose or fever does not by itself answer whether an antibiotic is needed. Use this sequence: 1. Identify the likely cause. Colds and influenza are viral; some conditions, such as streptococcal throat infection or bacterial pneumonia, may require antibacterial treatment. 2. Consider severity and duration. Symptoms that are severe, worsening, unusually prolonged or accompanied by red flags deserve medical assessment. 3. Use testing where it changes treatment. A throat swab, rapid test, urine test, culture or imaging may sometimes help distinguish causes. 4. Treat symptoms appropriately. Rest, fluids and suitable symptom relief may be the main care for an uncomplicated viral infection. 5. Use antibiotics only when prescribed for the current illness. The medicine, dose and duration depend on the suspected bacterium, infection site and patient. 6. Seek reassessment if the illness changes. A later bacterial complication is possible even when the original infection was viral. Do not use a leftover prescription as a test of whether an illness is bacterial. Improvement after taking it cannot establish that the medicine caused the recovery. Bacteria and viruses are different targets Bacteria are living cells. Depending on the drug class, an antibiotic may interfere with bacterial cell wall construction, protein synthesis, DNA replication or another process needed for bacterial growth and survival. Viruses are not bacterial cells. They consist of genetic material surrounded by proteins and, in some cases, a lipid envelope. They reproduce by entering host cells and using cellular machinery to make more virus. The bacterial targets attacked by penicillin and many other antibiotics simply are not present.[1][4] This is why antibiotics do not act as general “infection medicine”. Infection describes invasion by a microorganism; it does not identify which microorganism is responsible. Different pathogens require different approaches: Bacteria: antibiotics may work if the organism is susceptible and the medicine reaches the infection effectively. Viruses: some infections have specific antiviral medicines, while many are managed with supportive care as the immune system clears them. Fungi: antifungal medicines target fungal structures and metabolism. Parasites: treatment depends on the particular organism and life cycle. A drug can be valuable against one class of pathogen and useless against another. A key opening a bacterial lock does not fit a viral one. Antibiotics do not “boost” the immune system Another misconception is that an antibiotic gives the immune system extra strength regardless of cause. Antibiotics reduce susceptible bacteria; they do not generally strengthen immunity or help immune cells recognise a virus. Some antibiotics have anti inflammatory effects and are used in selected chronic conditions under specialist supervision. That does not turn them into treatments for ordinary viral colds or influenza. The indication, dose and treatment goal in those specialist uses are different. Illustration 1 Decision Path for Respiratory Illness Why colds and flu do not respond The common cold can be caused by many respiratory viruses. Influenza is caused by influenza viruses. Antibiotics cannot stop either virus from entering cells or reproducing.[5] For uncomplicated colds, the illness usually improves as the immune response controls the infection. Treatment centres on comfort, hydration and monitoring for complications. A Cochrane review found no benefit from antibiotics for the common cold or acute purulent rhinitis sufficient to justify routine use, while adverse effects were more common among those receiving antibiotics.[6] Acute bronchitis—sometimes called a chest cold—is also usually viral in otherwise healthy people. Cough can last for several weeks even after the infection begins to settle. That persistence does not by itself show that bacteria are present. A Cochrane review found at most limited average benefit from antibiotics for acute bronchitis, alongside increased adverse effects; the balance may differ in frail older people or others at higher risk who were under represented in trials.[7] Influenza may have specific antiviral treatment, particularly for people at higher risk or when treatment begins promptly. An antiviral is not an antibiotic, and its use depends on the virus, timing and clinical circumstances. Why recovery after antibiotics is misleading A familiar story goes like this: symptoms were severe, an antibiotic was started, and improvement followed. The conclusion seems obvious—but several alternatives can explain the sequence. The illness was already near its turning point People often seek treatment when symptoms are at their worst. Many self limiting illnesses would then improve naturally. This statistical tendency is known as regression towards the mean: unusually severe symptoms are likely to be followed by less severe ones even without a new treatment. If an antibiotic is started at the peak, natural recovery can be credited to the medicine. Other care began at the same time Rest, fluids, fever relief, sleep and reassurance may all accompany the prescription. Any improvement is attributed to the most medically distinctive action—the antibiotic—even though it may not have caused the change. The diagnosis may have been uncertain Clinicians sometimes prescribe empirically, meaning treatment begins before the precise organism is known. An antibiotic may be offered because a bacterial cause is plausible or the consequences of missing one are serious. If the patient improves, that does not retrospectively prove which organism was responsible. Why Antibiotics are Useless Against Viruses Channel: Microbehunter Open on YouTube A bacterial complication may have been present A respiratory virus can damage airway defences or alter local conditions, allowing a secondary bacterial infection to develop. In that situation, the antibiotic treats the bacterial complication, not the original virus. This distinction explains why both of these statements can be true: antibiotics do not cure influenza; a person with influenza may later need antibiotics for bacterial pneumonia. Symptoms alone can mislead Many viral and bacterial respiratory illnesses overlap. Fever, fatigue, swollen glands, coloured mucus, sore throat and cough are immune responses or signs of inflammation, not unique bacterial labels. Green mucus is not a bacterial diagnosis Yellow or green nasal mucus can result from immune cells and enzymes during either viral or bacterial illness. Colour alone does not show that antibiotics are needed. Clinicians consider the whole pattern, including duration, severity, facial pain, initial improvement followed by worsening and other findings.[8] A severe sore throat may still be viral Viruses cause most sore throats. Group A streptococcal infection is bacterial and may warrant antibiotics in selected patients, but cough, runny nose and hoarseness can point towards a viral cause. Where streptococcal infection is plausible, a clinical score or diagnostic test may help guide treatment.[9][10] Antibiotics should not be taken simply because throat pain is intense. Conversely, a severe or rapidly worsening illness should not be dismissed as viral without appropriate assessment. A long cough does not automatically need antibiotics Cough after acute bronchitis frequently lasts longer than patients expect. This mismatch between expected and normal duration can create pressure for antibiotics. Persistent cough may also have other causes, so duration should prompt reassessment where appropriate—not automatic antibacterial treatment.[7][11] Fever does not settle the question Both bacteria and viruses can produce high temperatures, and some serious bacterial infections may occur without"},{"title":"Corrections that stick","url":"/corrections-that-stick/","description":"A correction - a debunking, in the research vocabulary on misinformation; its pre-emptive cousin is prebunking - is most likely to stick when it gives...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Corrections that stick A correction a debunking, in the research vocabulary on misinformation; its pre emptive cousin is prebunking is most likely to stick when it gives... A correction a debunking, in the research vocabulary on misinformation; its pre emptive cousin is prebunking is most likely to stick when it gives the accurate account a clear advantage in attention, explanation and memory. Corrections that stick 2d9b64 Updated 2026 11 03 Introduction The shortest usable structure is: fact → warning → myth → explanation → fact . It is sometimes called a “truth sandwich”, although the label is a practical communication aid rather than a scientifically unique formula.[3] Research supports its main components: prominent facts, explicit falsehood labels, explanatory rebuttals and credible alternatives generally outperform vague denial.[4][5] No format guarantees permanent belief change. Corrections can fade, and the original claim may continue to shape reasoning. The aim is therefore not merely to say that a myth is wrong, but to make the accurate explanation easier to understand, retrieve and repeat. Build the correction in seven steps 1. Verify the correction before publishing it A correction loses value if it overstates the evidence, answers a different claim or requires its own correction later. Begin by reducing the disputed claim to a proposition that can actually be checked. Ask: What, exactly, is alleged to have happened? Which part is demonstrably false, misleading or unsupported? What evidence would establish that judgement? Is the evidence current enough for the claim’s time frame? Which details remain uncertain? This prevents a common error: rebutting an exaggerated version of the myth while leaving its real appeal untouched. If a circulating post says a medicine “always causes” a particular condition, evidence that the condition is uncommon addresses the universal claim. It does not prove that the event has never occurred. Precise correction is more credible than replacing one absolute with another. Distinguish fact from interpretation as well. A data source may establish that a rate fell during a particular period without establishing why it fell. A strong correction marks that boundary instead of using accurate statistics to imply an unsupported causal story. 2. Lead with the accurate account Put the fact in the headline, opening sentence or first visible panel. Readers should be able to understand the correction’s position without first absorbing a vivid restatement of the myth. Compare: Weak: “Did the new vaccine alter recipients’ DNA?” Stronger: “The vaccine does not alter a recipient’s DNA.” The second version reduces ambiguity and gives the accurate proposition the most prominent position. This matters online, where people may see only a headline, preview or screenshot. Leading with the truth does not mean hiding the myth completely. Readers need enough information to connect the correction with the claim they encountered. The practical goal is prominence, not avoidance: make the accurate account the organising idea and the myth a clearly labelled object of correction.[3][6] 3. Warn before naming the myth Introduce the false claim with an unmistakable cue: “A false post claims…”, “This figure is misleading because…”, or “There is no evidence for the allegation that…”. The warning helps bind the claim to its status. It also reduces the risk that someone skimming the page will mistake a repeated falsehood for the correction itself. Typography, captions and social media cards should preserve that relationship; a large myth quotation followed by a small correction can reverse the intended hierarchy. Use the myth’s essential wording once if recognition requires it. Avoid repeating dramatic slogans, hashtags or memorable false statistics throughout the piece merely for rhetorical effect. Repetition can increase familiarity, and familiarity can later be mistaken for truth, especially when people forget where they encountered the statement.[7][8] Yet this risk should not be exaggerated: clearly labelled repetition within an effective correction normally improves accuracy rather than producing routine backfire.[9] Illustration 1 Truth Sandwich Build 4. Diagnose the error, not just the verdict “False” is a conclusion. A useful correction shows how that conclusion was reached. The explanation might reveal that: a photograph comes from a different event; a chart begins at a misleading date; two percentages use different denominators; an observational association has been presented as proof of causation; a quotation omits the sentence that changes its meaning; an authentic document has been given an invented description; a real adverse event was reported, but reporting did not establish that the product caused it. Meta analytic evidence indicates that detailed counter messages tend to be more effective than bare negation.[4] Explaining the error gives readers a reason to revise their belief and a way to recognise the same tactic when the claim reappears. Keep the diagnostic step proportional. A long technical detour can make the correction harder to remember than the myth. State the decisive flaw first; supporting detail can follow for readers who want to inspect it. Debunking and Prebunking: How to Fight Misinformation Channel: Healthcare Triage Open on YouTube 5. Fill the explanatory gap A myth often survives because it supplies a complete story. A retraction removes a detail without replacing the role it played. Classic continued influence experiments used reports about a warehouse fire. Participants initially learnt that volatile materials had been found in a cupboard and were later told that this information was incorrect. Even people who remembered the retraction continued to refer to the materials when explaining the fire.[10] The false detail had provided a cause; after its removal, the story contained a hole. Corrections become more durable when they supply a plausible replacement. Experiments have found that additional explanations can reduce later reliance on retracted misinformation more effectively than denial alone.[11][12] A useful alternative need not explain everything, but it should answer the question the myth appeared to answer. For example: Fact: The surge in reported cases followed a change in testing, not a sudden change in the population’s underlying risk. False claim: Posts wrongly describe the higher count as proof that the condition suddenly became more common. Explanation: The new testing programme identified cases that previously went unrecorded. Rates calculated using a consistent method did not show the claimed jump. Fact: The reporting system changed; the available evidence does not show a sudden rise in underlying risk. The alternative must itself be supported. Inventing a neat explanation merely to compete with the myth substitutes one unsupported story for another. Where the cause is unknown, say so and explain what has been ruled out, what remains possible and what evidence would resolve the question. 6. Show the evidence and its provenance Readers should be able to see why the source is in a position to know. Name the relevant dataset, original document, investigation, image archive or specialist body. Link to primary material where practical, and distinguish a source’s expertise from its institutional prestige. Source credibility can affect correction, but its influence is not mechanically transferable. A trusted doctor may be persuasive about clinical evidence but not election administration. A statistical agency may be authoritative about a published series but not about the motives behind a policy. Research on political rumours and social media health misinformation suggests that recognised experts and unexpected or cross cutting messengers can improve correction in some settings.[13][14] Other studies find that the correction’s substance can matter more than the label attached to its source.[1] A robust correction therefore does not say only “experts disagree”. It makes the evidential path visible: 1. identify the source; 2. state what it measured or examined; 3. describe the finding relevant to the claim; 4. acknowledge material limitations; 5. provide access to the underlying evidence. This transparency also makes the correction more portable. Someone repeating it in another setting can preserve the reasoning rather than relying on a naked appeal to authority. 7. Reinforce the accurate account End with the fact in plain language. The final line should work even if separated from the rest of the correction. Repetition is useful when it strengthens the accurate account rather than repeatedly advertising the myth. Studies of misinformation and correction show that reminders can reduce continued influence, although no reminder permanently immunises memory against later exposure.[15] In fast moving situations, update the correction when new evidence appears and repeat it at the point"},{"title":"Do corrections work","url":"/do-corrections-work/","description":"Corrections usually work: after people see a credible factual correction, their belief in the false claim generally falls.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Do corrections work Corrections usually work: after people see a credible factual correction, their belief in the false claim generally falls. Corrections usually work: after people see a credible factual correction, their belief in the false claim generally falls. The original misinformation may still influence memory, interpretation or behaviour, and a correction may be weaker among people strongly committed to the claim. Do corrections work 8c1f47 Updated 2026 11 03 Introduction This distinction matters because exaggerated fear of backfire can discourage journalists, health authorities, teachers and ordinary users from correcting consequential errors. The evidence supports a more practical conclusion: correct misinformation promptly, use a clear and credible explanation, supply an accurate alternative, and do not mistake incomplete persuasion—or unchanged political loyalties—for proof that facts have made matters worse. Where the backfire claim came from The modern debate was shaped by Brendan Nyhan and Jason Reifler’s 2010 study of political misperceptions. In one experiment, politically conservative participants read a mock news report containing President George W. Bush’s assertion that Iraq possessed weapons of mass destruction before the 2003 invasion. A correction stated that no such weapons had been found. In a subgroup of strongly conservative participants, corrected readers reported greater belief in the false claim than comparable readers who had not received the correction. The researchers described this as a backfire effect.[4] The study deserved attention. It offered a psychologically plausible warning: if a correction threatens an important political identity, people may scrutinise it defensively and generate counterarguments. The finding also fitted a broader literature on motivated reasoning, in which preferences affect how evidence is evaluated. Yet a striking result from one set of experiments was soon converted into a sweeping public rule: do not correct myths, because repetition or contradiction will probably entrench them. That rule combined several different phenomena: Belief backfire: the correction causes greater belief in the false claim than an appropriate uncorrected comparison. Continued influence: belief declines, but the retracted information still affects reasoning. Partisan resistance: correction works less well for one political group than another. Attitude persistence: a factual belief changes, but an opinion about a politician or policy does not. Familiarity effects: repeating a claim makes it easier to recognise later, sometimes without preserving whether it was labelled true or false. Only the first is a backfire effect in the strict experimental sense. If belief falls from eight to five on a ten point scale, the correction has worked even if five remains uncomfortably high. If both political groups update but one moves less, that is asymmetric correction, not backfire. If voters accept a fact check but still support their preferred candidate, the correction changed the factual belief without changing the political judgement. Early practical advice did not always maintain these distinctions. The original Debunking Handbook , published in 2011, warned communicators about possible familiarity and worldview backfires and recommended avoiding unnecessary repetition of myths.[5] Those cautions were reasonable hypotheses given the evidence then available, but they acquired greater certainty in popular retellings than the underlying research justified. Illustration 1 Debunking Handbook Evolution What later experiments found Direct replications found a rare, fragile effect Thomas Wood and Ethan Porter conducted five experiments involving more than 10,000 participants and 52 politically contentious claims. Their corrections generally made beliefs more accurate, including when the correction challenged a participant’s ideological side. They found only one apparent backfire result, and it did not survive further investigation. Their conclusion was not that people reason without bias, but that factual backfire was far more elusive than its reputation suggested.[6] A separate re examination of the Iraq weapons experiment also failed to reproduce the famous result reliably. Luke Haglin found that the apparent effect depended heavily on how participants and responses were analysed; across broader specifications, correction did not consistently increase the misperception.[7] Later methodological reviews reached a similar judgement: documented cases exist, but many claims of backfire reflect small samples, subgroup analysis, ambiguous benchmarks or confusion between resistance and increased false belief.[8] This is an important statistical point. A correction should normally be compared with what an otherwise similar group believes without it. Observing that some corrected people remain mistaken—or even that their confidence increases between two measurements—does not establish that the correction caused the increase. Random assignment, sufficiently large samples and a clearly defined outcome are needed to separate a genuine causal backfire from ordinary variation. Meta analyses show correction gains, not routine reversal Research syntheses consistently find that corrective messages improve factual accuracy on average. Man pui Sally Chan and colleagues’ meta analysis of debunking studies found a substantial corrective effect, although misinformation often retained some influence. Corrections were more successful when they developed a detailed counter message, while effects were weaker when audiences had already generated reasons supporting the false account.[9] Nathan Walter and Sheila Murphy’s meta analysis likewise found that corrective messages reduced misperceptions, with outcomes shaped by features such as message coherence, audience predispositions and the subject being corrected.[10] A later meta analysis of political fact checking found an overall positive effect on beliefs, while also showing that the size of the gain varied with message design and political context.[11] These averages do not mean every correction works. Some are too vague, come from distrusted sources or arrive after a misleading explanation has become well integrated into memory. Some studies find effects that fade. The evidence nevertheless runs in the opposite direction from the popular warning that correction usually aggravates false belief. Why Proving Someone Wrong Makes Them MORE Certain (The Backfire Effect) Channel: Mind Unlocked Open on YouTube Corrections travel across political and national boundaries Evidence outside single country laboratory studies strengthens the conclusion. In simultaneous experiments conducted in Argentina, Nigeria, South Africa and the United Kingdom, Ethan Porter and Thomas Wood tested fact checks addressing locally relevant political misinformation. Accuracy improved across all four countries, despite differences in political institutions, media systems and public trust.[12] Studies of electoral misinformation reach a similarly qualified result. During France’s 2017 presidential campaign, factual rebuttals reduced acceptance of misleading claims made by Marine Le Pen, but they did not necessarily reduce political support for her.[13] In the United States, fact checks could improve assessments of claim accuracy without materially changing evaluations of the politician responsible.[14] These findings are sometimes described as evidence that fact checking “does not work”, but they show something narrower: changing a factual belief is easier than changing the larger political identity or value judgement surrounding it. Public health corrections also commonly improve accuracy, though durability is a real constraint. Experiments concerning COVID 19 misinformation in the United States, Britain and Canada found that fact checks reduced targeted misperceptions, but the benefits weakened over time and repeated exposure to misinformation could erode them.[15] This is a decay problem, not evidence that correction generally reverses direction. Why a corrected myth can still linger The failure of the backfire generalisation should not be replaced by the opposite myth that one correction erases an error. Research on the “continued influence effect” shows that retracted information can remain active in reasoning after people acknowledge that it was false.[16] A classic experimental example involved a news story about a warehouse fire. Participants initially learnt that volatile materials had been stored in a cupboard; a later message retracted that information. Even after remembering the retraction, participants continued to use the supposed materials when explaining the fire.[17] The false detail had supplied a causal story. Removing it left a gap, and memory did not automatically construct a replacement. Later experiments found that explicit warnings and repeated retractions can reduce this residual"},{"title":"Fact checks and votes","url":"/fact-checks-and-votes/","description":"Fact-checks often make political beliefs more accurate without changing how people vote.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Fact checks and votes Fact checks often make political beliefs more accurate without changing how people vote. Fact checks often make political beliefs more accurate without changing how people vote. The evidence for this pattern is reasonably strong: experiments conducted during elections have repeatedly found that participants accept at least part of a correction while retaining much the same view of the candidate or policy involved. Fact checks and votes 7a3e58 Updated 2026 11 03 Introduction Voters weigh party loyalty, values, group identity, economic interests, trust, candidate character and comparisons with the alternative. Correcting one campaign claim changes only one input into that larger judgement. Effects on votes are possible, especially when corrected information is important, credible and previously unknown, but they are generally smaller and less consistent than effects on factual accuracy. Most importantly, unchanged support is not a backfire effect unless the correction actually increases belief in the false claim. The verdict in practical terms The best supported assessment is that fact checking is primarily an accuracy intervention, not a dependable vote switching device. It can help citizens distinguish substantiated claims from false ones, but the research does not justify promising that a successful correction will alter candidate support, policy preferences or election results. Three qualifications keep that conclusion from becoming another myth. First, an average effect is not a universal rule. Some corrections do affect candidate evaluations or voting intentions, particularly when the disputed fact is central to the decision.[4][5] Secondly, most studies measure immediate survey responses rather than verified votes cast weeks or months later. That makes the evidence about beliefs much stronger than the evidence about final electoral behaviour. Thirdly, a small average voting effect could still matter in a close election, even when it is difficult to detect in an individual experiment. The practical distinction is: Correction succeeds: belief in the false claim decreases. Correction has no attitudinal spillover: candidate or policy support remains similar. Correction backfires: belief in the false claim becomes stronger than it would have been without correction. Only the third result supports a factual backfire claim. Political disappointment with the first two does not change their meaning. Experiments test two different outcomes Researchers can separate factual updating from political persuasion by asking different questions after randomly assigning participants to receive a correction or a control message. A belief measure might ask whether crime rose, whether unemployment reached a stated level or whether a candidate supported a particular proposal. An attitudinal measure asks how favourably the participant views the candidate, which policy they prefer or how likely they are to vote for someone. Random assignment then reveals whether the correction changed either outcome relative to people who did not see it. This separation matters because factual questions and political choices have different structures. A factual proposition can often be answered largely by evidence: the official rate was one figure rather than another. Candidate choice is comparative and multi dimensional. A voter can concede that a preferred candidate used a false statistic yet still prefer that candidate’s policies, party, appointments or perceived character to those of the opponent. Survey responses also contain complications. Some respondents use factual questions partly to express partisan allegiance. Studies offering small payments for accurate answers have found that partisan gaps sometimes shrink, indicating that at least some apparently false answers represent cheerleading or expressive responding rather than firmly held factual beliefs.[6][7] Fact checking may therefore change knowledge, willingness to report that knowledge, or both. Neither automatically dictates a vote. Illustration 1 Fact Check Impact Pathway Trump corrections changed accuracy more than support A particularly clear demonstration came from Briony Swire and colleagues during Donald Trump’s rise in US politics. Participants evaluated several claims attributed to Trump and then saw corrections. The corrections made assessments of those claims more accurate, including among Trump supporters. Yet the factual updating produced little corresponding movement in voting intentions. Supporters could recognise that particular statements were wrong without withdrawing their broader support.[1] Brendan Nyhan, Ethan Porter, Jason Reifler and Thomas Wood later examined journalistic fact checking of Trump during the 2016 presidential campaign. Across three experiments, fact checks generally improved factual accuracy. The gains were greater among people predisposed to reject Trump, but his supporters also became more accurate. Candidate favourability moved little.[2] The result is easy to misread. It did not show that Trump supporters were literally impervious to evidence: their factual responses changed in the predicted direction. Nor did it show that corrections strengthened the false claims. It showed that candidate evaluation was buffered from corrections to individual statements. That buffer can take several forms. A supporter may consider the falsehood unimportant, regard it as ordinary political exaggeration, distrust the fact checker’s framing or compare it with perceived faults in the opposing candidate. The person may also substitute another justification: “That statistic was wrong, but the underlying problem is real.” Researchers sometimes call this process belief bolstering or rationalisation, but observing stable support alone cannot establish which mechanism operated. France showed the same belief vote gap A large experiment linked to France’s 2017 presidential election tested false or misleading statistical claims used by Marine Le Pen. Oscar Barrera and colleagues compared exposure to her claims, official statistics and fact checks that paired the claims with corrections.[3] The corrections improved factual knowledge. Participants became better able to identify the correct figures. But correcting the numbers did not reliably reverse the broader political consequences of encountering Le Pen’s arguments, including voting intentions. Accurate statistics and electoral preference followed different paths. This case illustrates why “providing the facts” can be necessary without being sufficient. A disputed statistic may support a campaign narrative—about immigration, unemployment or public services—but it is rarely the narrative’s only support. A voter who accepts the corrected number may retain the same moral priority, perception of social change or distrust of established parties. The correction removes one piece of evidence without necessarily displacing the larger interpretation. The French study also cautions against evaluating fact checks by a standard they were not designed to meet. If a correction accurately repairs knowledge, calling it ineffective because it does not determine a vote collapses civic information and electoral persuasion into one task. Evaluating Sources & Fact Checking: Crash Course Scientific Thinking 6 Channel: CrashCourse Open on YouTube Corrected claims can leave “belief echoes” Emily Thorson’s research on “belief echoes” offers another explanation for the gap. In her experiments, participants encountered negative political information that was later corrected. Corrections could repair the explicit factual belief while leaving behind a less favourable attitude towards the person targeted by the misinformation.[8] One route is cognitive: even after a retraction, the initial claim may continue to influence the story people use to explain events. Another is emotional: disgust, anger or suspicion generated by the allegation may outlast memory for its evidential status. A voter may no longer endorse the exact accusation yet retain a vague sense that something troubling surrounds the candidate. Belief echoes are not inevitable, and their size depends on the message and experimental conditions. They nevertheless show why asking only “Do you now believe the claim?” can miss downstream residue. Conversely, asking only “Do you still dislike the candidate?” can wrongly imply that the correction failed to improve factual understanding. This is related to the broader continued influence effect, in which retracted information remains available in memory and reasoning.[9][10] But political attitude stability does not require continued false belief. A person can accurately remember the correction and still decide that it carries little weight in the final choice. Why votes are harder to move than factual answers Several forces dilute the journey from a corrected fact to a changed vote. A vote combines many considerations. A fact check normally"},{"title":"Health myths real decisions","url":"/health-myths-real-decisions/","description":"Health myths become dangerous when they move from screens into decisions: declining vaccination, demanding antibiotics for viral illness, spending money...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Health myths real decisions Health myths become dangerous when they move from screens into decisions: declining vaccination, demanding antibiotics for viral illness, spending money... Health myths become dangerous when they move from screens into decisions: declining vaccination, demanding antibiotics for viral illness, spending money on unsupported “brain training”, or delaying appropriate care. The evidence does not suggest that everyone who encounters misinformation acts on it. Health myths real decisions 4b6d92 Updated 2026 11 03 Introduction Three case families make the pattern clear. The discredited claim linking the measles, mumps and rubella vaccine to autism survived retraction and repeated contrary studies. Confusion about viruses and bacteria encourages antibiotic use that cannot treat the underlying infection and contributes to antimicrobial resistance. Brain myths cause less immediate physical harm, but can distort education, consumer choices and expectations of treatment. In each case, the durable myth starts with a fragment of genuine scientific language and extends it far beyond what the evidence supports. The vaccine autism claim outlived its source The modern measles, mumps and rubella vaccine scare is closely associated with a 1998 paper in The Lancet . The paper described 12 children and suggested that their developmental and intestinal symptoms might be connected with the vaccine. Its design could not establish that vaccination caused autism: it had no appropriate control group, relied heavily on retrospective accounts and involved a tiny, selected case series. The paper was partially retracted in 2004 and fully retracted by The Lancet in 2010.[3] A detailed BMJ investigation by journalist Brian Deer reported discrepancies between the published descriptions and the children’s medical records, along with undisclosed conflicts surrounding the work.[4] Retraction removed the paper from the reliable scientific record, but it could not remove the story from public memory. The claim had several qualities that make a medical myth resilient: it named a feared outcome, appeared to identify a specific trigger and offered parents an explanation for changes noticed during early childhood. The timing was emotionally persuasive even though temporal sequence alone could not establish causation. Larger studies found no increased autism risk Subsequent research has examined far larger populations using designs better suited to detecting an association. A Danish nationwide cohort study followed 657,461 children born between 1999 and 2010. It found no increased risk of autism after measles, mumps and rubella vaccination, including among subgroups thought to be at increased risk.[5] A meta analysis covering cohort and case control studies involving more than 1.2 million children likewise found no association between vaccination and autism or autistic spectrum disorder.[6] Researchers have also studied children with an older sibling who has autism, a group whose families may be especially concerned about risk. A US study published in JAMA found no harmful association between measles, mumps and rubella vaccination and autism diagnosis in children with or without an older autistic sibling.[7] No observational study can prove a universal negative in the abstract. The relevant conclusion is that large, appropriately designed studies have repeatedly failed to find the claimed association, while the paper that launched the scare was small, methodologically incapable of proving causation and retracted. Real side effects do not rescue a false claim Vaccines, like other medical products, can cause side effects. Most are mild and temporary; rare serious reactions are monitored through clinical studies and post authorisation surveillance.[8] Acknowledging those risks is part of accurate communication, not a concession to the autism claim. A sound risk comparison asks: What adverse event is being claimed? How frequently does it occur after vaccination? Does it occur more often than the expected background rate? Is there a plausible and supported causal mechanism? What are the risks of the disease vaccination prevents? An adverse event reported after vaccination is not automatically caused by it. When millions of people are vaccinated, illnesses and diagnoses will occur afterwards by coincidence. Safety systems use comparison groups, observed versus expected analyses and clinical assessment to distinguish signals from background events. Illustration 1 MMR Vaccine Retraction Time line The public health consequence is collective Vaccine decisions affect more than one individual because infectious diseases spread between people. Measles is exceptionally contagious, and the World Health Organization identifies high, sustained vaccination coverage as essential to preventing outbreaks.[9] Exposure to alarming vaccine content can shift perceived risk and willingness to vaccinate. Experiments involving vaccine critical websites found that even brief exposure increased perceptions of vaccination risk and reduced intention to vaccinate.[10] During the COVID 19 pandemic, experimental exposure to vaccine misinformation reduced stated vaccination intentions in both the United Kingdom and United States, although intentions are not identical to actual uptake.[2] The practical mistake is to compare a vivid hypothetical vaccine injury with an invisible alternative in which infection is prevented. A balanced decision compares known vaccine benefits and risks with the probability and consequences of remaining susceptible to disease. Antibiotics do not treat viral infections Antibiotics act against bacteria through mechanisms such as disrupting bacterial cell walls, protein production or replication. Viruses have different structures and reproduce inside host cells. Antibiotics therefore do not treat the viruses responsible for colds, influenza and many sore throats, coughs and cases of acute bronchitis.[11][12] This distinction sounds simple, but several experiences make the misconception persuasive. A person may take an antibiotic for a viral illness and recover several days later. The recovery feels like proof, even though many viral infections resolve through the immune response over the same period. A prescription may also coincide with rest, fluids and symptom relief. Sometimes the picture is genuinely complicated. A viral infection can be followed by a bacterial complication, and similar symptoms can have different causes. The correct lesson is not that antibiotics are never appropriate for respiratory symptoms; it is that symptoms alone do not justify assuming a bacterial infection. Clinical assessment and, in some cases, diagnostic testing determine whether antibacterial treatment is likely to help. Physicians tackle equitable vaccine distribution/misinformation COVID 19 Update for March 4, 2021 Channel: American Medical Association (AMA) Open on YouTube Why “just in case” has a cost Taking an unnecessary antibiotic offers no benefit against the virus but can still cause diarrhoea, allergic reactions, drug interactions and other adverse effects. It also exposes bacteria to the medicine, selecting for resistant strains. Antimicrobial resistance occurs when microorganisms evolve so that medicines no longer work effectively against them; it is the organisms, not the patient, that become resistant.[13] Resistance changes future treatment. An infection that was once straightforward may require a different medicine, intravenous treatment or hospital care. Resistant organisms can also spread to other people. The decision to use an antibiotic therefore has both immediate and population level consequences. A systematic review of public knowledge and expectations found persistent misunderstandings about antibiotics, including uncertainty about whether they work against viruses and expectations that clinicians should prescribe them for respiratory illness.[14] Studies of outpatient prescribing have also documented substantial inappropriate antibiotic use, although estimates depend on setting, diagnostic coding and the standard applied.[15] The myths run in both directions Public health messages sometimes replace “antibiotics cure everything” with rules that are almost as crude. “Never take antibiotics for a cough” is unsafe because some coughs reflect bacterial pneumonia or another condition requiring treatment. “Always finish every antibiotic course no matter what” also removes clinical context. The appropriate rule is to take the medicine exactly as currently prescribed and seek advice before stopping, extending or reusing it. Other useful distinctions are: Feeling better does not prove the original illness was bacterial. Green mucus alone does not establish that an antibiotic is required. A strong antibiotic is not necessarily a better antibiotic. Treatment should target the likely organism and infection site. Leftover tablets are not a diagnostic test. They"},{"title":"How to check a claim","url":"/how-to-check-a-claim/","description":"Before sharing a doubtful claim, pause and run four checks: define it, trace it, test it and compare it - the fact-checking routine that professional...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"How to check a claim Before sharing a doubtful claim, pause and run four checks: define it, trace it, test it and compare it the fact checking routine that professional... Before sharing a doubtful claim, pause and run four checks: define it, trace it, test it and compare it the fact checking routine that professional fact checkers call lateral reading. Write down the exact factual assertion. Trace the quotation, image, statistic or study to its earliest available source. How to check a claim 3e5f97 Updated 2026 11 03 Introduction This approach is called lateral reading: instead of judging a page mainly by its appearance, open other sources to investigate who produced it and how its claims compare with the wider record.[1][2] Professional fact checkers use this habit more effectively than readers who remain on an unfamiliar website inspecting its design or “About” page.[3] Verification need not settle every dispute. Its immediate purpose is to decide whether the evidence justifies sharing, whether the claim needs qualification, or whether the safest and most accurate action is to wait. The quickest reliable check For a low stakes claim, use this five minute sequence: 1. State the claim in one sentence. Remove emotional framing and identify what could be true or false. 2. Search a distinctive phrase. Put a quotation or unusual wording into a search engine to locate earlier versions. 3. Open the original source. Find the complete speech, study, dataset, photograph, ruling or announcement. 4. Read laterally. Open new tabs to investigate the publisher, author and claim elsewhere. 5. Check date and context. Confirm that the material concerns the same place, period, population and event. 6. Seek independent confirmation. Look for sources that did not simply copy one another. 7. Decide proportionately. Share only what the evidence supports, with uncertainty and source links preserved. Mike Caulfield’s widely used SIFT method compresses this into four moves: stop; investigate the source; find better coverage; and trace claims, quotations and media to their original context.[1] The order matters. Do not spend ten minutes analysing a polished page if a thirty second search reveals that it is a spoof site, an undisclosed lobbying project or a copy of an old rumour. High stakes claims deserve more than a quick check. Medical advice, financial allegations, election information, emergencies and accusations against identifiable people may require primary records, specialist expertise and confirmation from several independent sources. Define what is actually being claimed A post can mix observation, inference, prediction and opinion so smoothly that the checkable proposition disappears. Verification begins by separating them. Consider: “This hospital is hiding a huge rise in vaccine injuries.” It may contain several distinct assertions: a particular hospital recorded more incidents; the increase was unusually large; the incidents were caused by vaccination; officials concealed the information; the pattern applies beyond that hospital. Evidence for one assertion does not establish the rest. A rise in reports is not automatically a rise in confirmed injuries; a temporal sequence does not establish causation; and a delayed publication is not by itself evidence of concealment. Rewrite the claim in neutral language, including its implied quantity and time frame. “A hospital recorded 300 suspected reactions during June, three times the previous month’s total” is checkable. “Officials are finally being exposed” is a conclusion looking for supporting evidence. Identify the burden of proof Ask what would have to be true for the claim to hold. A universal statement such as “this treatment never works” requires different evidence from “this trial found no benefit”. An accusation of deliberate fabrication requires evidence of knowledge and intent, not merely proof that a statement was inaccurate. Also check whether the claim has shifted. Someone may begin with “this photograph is genuine”, retreat to “the event could have happened”, and then treat the second proposition as vindication of the first. Verification should follow the original claim, not a weaker substitute introduced after contrary evidence appears. Illustration 1 Lateral Reading Workflow Trace the claim to its origin Online claims often arrive as screenshots, cropped charts, reposted videos or articles describing other articles. Each retelling can remove a date, qualification or source link. Work backwards until reaching the earliest accessible evidence. For text, search an unusual sentence or a sequence of distinctive words. If the wording is attributed to a person, locate the full transcript, recording, court document or original publication. A quotation database, social media graphic or news report may help locate the source, but it is not equivalent to the source itself. For statistics, locate the dataset or report that defined the measure. Check whether a headline’s “50 per cent increase” means a rise from two cases to three, whether the figure is adjusted for population size, and whether the comparison uses consistent methods. The denominator—what the number is divided by—is often more revealing than the percentage. For scientific findings, follow the chain to the paper or registered report. Confirm that the headline describes the measured outcome rather than a speculative implication. Search the title or digital object identifier in Crossref and the relevant scholarly database.[4][5] Then check for corrections, expressions of concern, retractions or substantive criticism, including entries in the Retraction Watch database and discussion on PubPeer.[6][7] An origin is not automatically trustworthy. Finding it tells you what evidence actually exists and what has been added in transmission. Leave the page and read laterally Traditional website checklists often encourage readers to inspect spelling, design, contact details or the presence of references. These cues are weak. Professional looking misinformation can satisfy them, while an expert’s plain institutional page may look dated. Lateral reading means leaving the unfamiliar page almost immediately. Search for the organisation, author, funder and claim in separate tabs. Researchers comparing professional fact checkers with historians and university students found that fact checkers reached sounder judgements partly because they investigated websites through outside sources rather than remaining within the sites themselves.[2][3] Useful lateral questions include: Who operates or funds the publisher? Does it have relevant expertise or direct access to the evidence? How do independent sources describe it? Has it issued corrections? Does the named author exist and have relevant experience? Is the article reporting original work or recycling another outlet? Is the source selling a product or campaigning for an outcome connected to the claim? Source investigation should inform how much weight to give the evidence, not become guilt by association. An advocacy group can publish accurate data; an established institution can make an error. The decisive question remains whether the evidence supports the claim. Research in schools and universities suggests that lateral reading instruction can improve online source evaluation.[8][9] It is more useful than memorising long lists of superficial warning signs because it directs attention to the network of evidence surrounding a source. Intro to Lateral Reading Teaching Online Fact Checking Channel: icivics Open on YouTube Check evidence, context and date Finding a real source is only halfway. Genuine material can be used misleadingly. Does the source say what the post says? Read beyond the title and abstract. Look for the exact passage, figure or table that supposedly supports the claim. A news post may turn “associated with” into “caused by”, “may” into “will”, or “no statistically significant difference” into “proof of no effect”. For studies, check: Population: Who was studied, and does the claim generalise beyond them? Sample size: Was the study large enough to estimate the effect reliably? Comparison: Was there a suitable control or reference group? Outcome: Did researchers measure the claimed real world result or only a proxy? Time frame: Was the follow up long enough? Uncertainty: How wide were the confidence intervals, and were plausible alternatives discussed? Design: Was the evidence observational, experimental, modelled or anecdotal? Publication status: Is it a preprint, peer reviewed article, correction or retracted work? One study rarely settles a broad scientific question. Systematic reviews assess multiple studies using explicit methods and are usually more informative than selecting whichever paper supports a preferred answer.[10] Even a review must be checked for scope, quality and currency. Is the material still current? Old content regularly returns during new events. A genuine"},{"title":"Myth check","url":"/myth-check/","description":"Myths and misconceptions are not simply foolish beliefs held by poorly informed people. They are ordinary products of human memory, reasoning and social life.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Myth check Myths and misconceptions are not simply foolish beliefs held by poorly informed people. They are ordinary products of human memory, reasoning and social life. Myths and misconceptions are not simply foolish beliefs held by poorly informed people. They are ordinary products of human memory, reasoning and social life. A myth is usually a widely shared explanatory story or claim; a misconception is an inaccurate understanding held by an individual or group. Myth check 0f4e9a Updated 2026 11 03 Introduction The evidence offers two important corrections to popular thinking about false beliefs. First, people are neither infinitely gullible nor immune to error: plausibility, repetition, emotion, prior knowledge and trust in the source all affect what seems true. Secondly, debunking generally works. Corrections can leave traces of the original claim behind, but the much publicised idea that fact checking routinely makes people cling more fiercely to falsehoods is itself misleading.[3][4] The practical lesson is to replace a myth with a clear, memorable explanation—not merely to label it wrong. What counts as a myth or misconception? The word “myth” has several meanings. It can describe a traditional story that conveys cultural meaning without functioning as literal history. It can also mean a popular but unsupported factual claim, such as “humans use only ten per cent of their brains”. Calling a cultural narrative a myth therefore does not automatically mean that it is worthless or deceptive. Confusion arises when these different meanings are treated as interchangeable. A misconception is narrower: it is a mistaken belief or mental model. Someone who thinks antibiotics kill viruses has misunderstood how the drugs work. A rumour, by contrast, is an unverified claim circulating between people. “Misinformation” normally means false or misleading information shared without regard to the sender’s intention, while “disinformation” refers to material deliberately created or distributed to deceive.[2][5] “Malinformation” is sometimes used for genuine information deployed misleadingly or maliciously, such as material stripped of context. These categories describe information, not permanent types of person. The same individual may carefully check a medical claim yet accept a flattering political statistic without scrutiny. Moreover, an inaccurate statement is not necessarily a lie: lying requires knowledge and intent that cannot be inferred from falsity alone. Why false ideas can feel true People cannot investigate every proposition from first principles. Everyday judgement therefore relies on useful shortcuts: Does the claim fit existing knowledge? Is it easy to understand? Have several apparently independent sources repeated it? Does it come from someone trusted? These shortcuts usually make communication possible, but they can also make a weak claim feel familiar and credible. Repetition creates familiarity, not certainty Experiments have repeatedly found an “illusory truth effect”: previously encountered statements tend to receive higher truth ratings than unfamiliar ones.[6][7] The effect can occur even when people possess relevant knowledge, because familiarity makes a statement easier to process.[8] That does not mean repetition hypnotises an audience or guarantees belief. Warnings, attentive reasoning, implausibility and strong contrary knowledge can limit its influence.[9] This distinction matters online. A reader may encounter the same assertion in a headline, a repost, a search snippet and a family message. What feels like confirmation by four sources may be one unsupported claim copied four times. Repetition is therefore a reason to locate the original evidence, not evidence in itself. Illustration 1 Defining Information Disorder Types Memory keeps the claim more easily than its status Human memory does not preserve a perfect recording with a permanent “true” or “false” label attached. People may remember the substance of a claim while forgetting that it came from satire, an unreliable source or a subsequent correction. Researchers call the lingering influence of retracted information the “continued influence effect”.[1][10] A classic experimental pattern involves an unfolding report—for example, a warehouse fire initially attributed to flammable materials. Even after that explanation is explicitly withdrawn, participants may use it when reasoning about the event if no satisfactory alternative is supplied.[11] Retraction removes an explanation but can leave a gap in the story. A replacement account helps because it gives memory something coherent to use instead. Emotion and identity influence attention Emotionally charged information receives attention and is more likely to be passed on. In one large analysis of Twitter data, messages containing moral emotional language spread more widely within political networks.[12] That finding does not prove that every emotional claim is false; accurate warnings and justified anger can also be emotionally powerful. It shows why vivid anecdotes often outcompete cautious explanations. Identity matters in a similarly qualified way. People may scrutinise information that threatens their group more harshly than information that flatters it. Yet research also shows that simple inattention and failure to consider accuracy explain a substantial share of low quality sharing.[13] Not every mistaken post is an ideological declaration. Eight myths about misinformation 1. “People believe almost everything they see online” People are often sceptical, especially towards unfamiliar sources. Exposure to fabricated news is also highly uneven. Research on the 2016 United States election found that consumption of identified false news websites was concentrated among a relatively small segment of the population rather than evenly distributed among all internet users.[14][15] This is reassuring but not grounds for complacency. A claim need not persuade everyone to cause harm; it may only need to reach the right community at a vulnerable moment. The more accurate picture is selective credulity: people reject much of what they encounter but can accept claims that seem plausible, familiar, socially endorsed or useful. Why people fall for misinformation Joseph Isaac Channel: TED Ed Open on YouTube 2. “False stories always spread faster than truth” A prominent study of about 126,000 stories circulated on Twitter between 2006 and 2017 found that false news travelled farther, faster and more broadly than true news in that dataset, with novelty and human sharing playing important roles.[16] The study is significant, but its result is often overgeneralised. It does not establish an iron law covering every platform, language, subject or definition of falsehood. Platform design, recommendation systems, breaking news conditions and the availability of authoritative information all affect diffusion. “Falsehood always wins” is therefore another myth; the defensible conclusion is that some digital environments reward novelty in ways that can advantage false claims. 3. “Only unintelligent or uneducated people fall for myths” Education and analytical reasoning can improve discernment, but neither confers immunity. Experts can reason poorly outside their speciality, while intelligent people may become skilled at constructing arguments for conclusions they already prefer. Familiarity, time pressure, identity and misplaced trust affect highly educated people too.[17][18] The stereotype is also counterproductive. Treating correction as a contest between the clever and the foolish makes error socially costly to admit. A better approach treats revising a belief as normal evidence of learning. 4. “A correction merely repeats and strengthens the myth” Older guidance sometimes warned communicators never to repeat a false claim because repetition might embed it in memory. Repetition can indeed create familiarity, so careless amplification is a real risk. But evidence does not support a blanket prohibition. Corrections must often identify the claim clearly enough for readers to recognise what is being corrected.[3][19] Effective formats lead with the accurate account, mention the false claim once and conspicuously label it false, explain why it fails, and finish by reinforcing the accurate account.[3] The danger comes from making the myth more vivid than the correction, not from naming it at all. Archive map 2016 United States House of Representatives elections in Ohio results map by county Nahida · Own work · 2025 05 09 · 164992245 Archival image related to 2016 United States House of Representatives elections in Ohio results map by county. CC0 · View original record 5. “Corrections usually backfire” The “backfire effect” is the claim that contradiction commonly causes people—particularly political partisans—to strengthen their original belief. An influential early study reported examples under particular conditions.[20] Later"},{"title":"Repeated messages and false consensus","url":"/repeated-messages-and-false-consensus/","description":"Repeated messages can create a false impression of social consensus, especially when their common origin is hidden.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Repeated messages and false consensus Repeated messages can create a false impression of social consensus, especially when their common origin is hidden. Repeated messages can create a false impression of social consensus, especially when their common origin is hidden. The evidence for this mechanism is strong in controlled experiments and plausible in digital networks, but it does not mean that users automatically believe whatever appears popular. Repeated messages and false consensus 9c6f12 Updated 2026 11 03 Introduction Online feeds make that confusion easier. One assertion may be copied by news aggregators, influencers, automated accounts and friends, then recommended repeatedly because it is already attracting engagement. Visible likes and shares add a second popularity signal. Yet ten encounters may still trace back to one anonymous post, one press release or one misread study. The practical test is therefore not how many times a claim appears, but how many genuinely independent and competent sources support it. Why one voice can sound like many People often use apparent consensus as evidence because agreement among independent observers can be informative. If several qualified teams examine the same question separately and reach similar conclusions, their convergence is more valuable than one team repeating itself. The shortcut fails when repeated exposure is mistaken for independent agreement. Kimberlee Weaver and colleagues demonstrated this in experiments in which participants encountered opinions attributed either to several people or repeatedly to one person. Repetition from the same speaker made an opinion seem more popular, even when participants could identify the repeated source.[1] The study’s memorable conclusion was that a repetitive voice can sound like a chorus. The mechanism combines two judgements: Familiarity: repeated statements are easier to process and can feel more credible.[3][4] Apparent prevalence: information encountered often can seem widely accepted, because frequent exposure normally correlates with many people discussing it. These judgements are related but not identical. Someone might remain unsure whether a claim is true while becoming convinced that “everyone is talking about it” or “most people believe it”. That perceived norm can then influence attention, willingness to speak and subsequent sharing. Online credibility research shows that users frequently rely on visible social endorsements when they lack the time or expertise to inspect underlying evidence. Endorsements can sometimes outweigh the political identity of a source when people select news, while popularity cues such as ratings and recommendations can serve as quick credibility heuristics.[5][6] These shortcuts are not always mistaken: other users may have identified genuinely useful material. The risk lies in treating volume as proof without checking how that volume was produced. Illustration 1 From Single Post to Crowd Illusion How feeds multiply a single claim A digital platform does more than transmit messages. It selects, ranks and repeats them. If a claim generates strong reactions, recommendation systems may show it to more people; those people may then share screenshots, commentary or rebuttals that reproduce the original assertion. The result can look like independent corroboration even when every path begins at the same source. Several processes contribute: Copying hides ancestry. Articles may cite another article rather than the original document. Posts lose links when they become screenshots. Videos are clipped and re uploaded. After several steps, the shared origin is difficult to see. Engagement creates repeated exposure. Highly discussed material appears in trending panels, recommendation feeds and replies. Its visibility can reflect conflict or curiosity rather than agreement. Social proof affects later reactions. In a large field experiment, an early positive rating made subsequent users more likely to rate a comment positively, producing a lasting upward distortion.[7] Experiments with artificial music markets likewise found that seeing other people’s choices increased inequality and made popularity less predictable: early advantages could compound.[8] Social influence narrows apparent diversity. In an experiment involving estimates of factual quantities, exposure to other participants’ answers reduced the diversity of responses without reliably improving collective accuracy. The group became more confident while preserving some errors.[9] Apparent convergence was therefore not equivalent to better evidence. Automation increases output without adding witnesses. Studies of low credibility material on Twitter found that automated accounts played a substantial role in amplifying links, particularly early in their diffusion.[10] Automated posting does not create additional independent evidence; it creates additional instances of the same message. These mechanisms can interact. A small group repeats a claim, automated or highly active accounts raise its visibility, recommendation systems surface the resulting engagement, and ordinary users encounter it through several apparently separate contacts. The final audience sees a crowd but not the copying process that assembled it. What is the False Consensus Effect? (Easiest Explanation) Channel: Social Science Explainer Open on YouTube Echo chambers are real but not total Research supports concern about clustered information environments, but stronger popular claims—such as “everyone lives in a sealed bubble”—go beyond the evidence. Studies of online misinformation have found communities that selectively consume and circulate content consistent with shared narratives.[11] Cross platform research has also documented echo chamber patterns, although their strength varies between services because platforms have different network structures and recommendation systems.[12] Analysis of Facebook news exposure has found substantial ideological segregation, including asymmetries in which groups encountered or shared different sources.[13][14] However, several qualifications matter. First, online audiences are not uniformly isolated. Search engines, news aggregators and social networks can expose people to sources they would not deliberately visit. Research on web browsing behaviour has found both ideological segregation and increased exposure to cross cutting material, depending on the route through which news was reached.[15][16] Secondly, exposure is highly concentrated. Studies of fabricated or low quality political news have found that a relatively small minority accounts for a large share of consumption and sharing.[17][18] A claim that dominates one network may be invisible to most of the public. Thirdly, exposure is not belief. A user may see a post because friends are mocking it, journalists are correcting it or opponents are criticising it. Engagement counts combine approval, disbelief, outrage and entertainment. Treating all interaction as endorsement inflates the apparent consensus twice: once in the platform’s ranking and again in the observer’s interpretation. Finally, network similarity does not prove that the network caused an opinion. People choose contacts and sources partly because of prior preferences. Large scale experiments and observational studies can estimate aspects of platform influence, but separating selection from persuasion remains difficult.[19][20] The most defensible assessment is therefore conditional: social platforms can make repeated claims look more widespread and independently supported than they are, but the scale and persuasive effect depend on the platform, community, topic and user. Illustration 2 Familiarity vs. Independent Agreement Evidence for a claim or merely evidence of circulation? A repeated message should count as independent confirmation only when its evidential path is independent. Three newspapers reporting the same allegation from one unnamed official are not three witnesses. Twenty posts quoting one withdrawn paper do not create twenty studies. A practical source audit can distinguish corroboration from copying: 1. Locate the earliest reachable version. Search distinctive wording, image details or quotations. Repeatedly identical phrasing is a clue that sources are copying rather than confirming. 2. Follow citations to primary material. Open the study, dataset, court filing, recording or official document. A circular chain—A cites B, B cites C, and C cites A—provides no independent foundation. 3. Count evidence producing sources. Ask who directly observed, measured or authenticated the matter. Commentators and republishers may help interpret evidence, but they do not become new witnesses by repeating it. 4. Separate agreement from engagement. Likes, views and reposts measure attention under platform specific conditions. They are not representative opinion polls and do not establish factual accuracy. 5. Check whether"},{"title":"Studies versus headlines","url":"/studies-versus-headlines/","description":"To test whether a study supports a headline, compare the strength of the headline’s claim with what the researchers actually did and measured.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Studies versus headlines To test whether a study supports a headline, compare the strength of the headline’s claim with what the researchers actually did and measured. To test whether a study supports a headline, compare the strength of the headline’s claim with what the researchers actually did and measured. Find the original paper, identify its design, population, comparison and main outcome, then check the size and uncertainty of the result. Studies versus headlines 5c4a26 Updated 2026 11 03 Introduction Do not stop at the abstract or press release. Look for the registered primary outcome, absolute numbers, limitations, corrections and later reviews. Research has found that exaggeration in science news is often already present in institutional press releases, particularly when causal language or human advice is stronger than the underlying study permits.[1][2] A headline need not be entirely fabricated to mislead; it may report a genuine finding while quietly enlarging what that finding means. The one minute headline test Before reading the entire paper, translate the headline into a precise claim and compare it with the study’s basic structure. Ask six questions: 1. Who was studied? Humans, animals, cells, survey respondents or records? 2. What was done? Was an intervention assigned, or were existing differences merely observed? 3. What was compared? Treatment versus placebo, high exposure versus low exposure, or one time period versus another? 4. What was measured? Illness, behaviour, survival, a questionnaire score or a laboratory marker? 5. How large was the result? What were the absolute numbers, not just the percentage change? 6. How uncertain was it? What range of effects remains compatible with the data? Now compare the verbs. “Was associated with”, “predicted”, “reduced in a laboratory model” and “caused” are not interchangeable. If an observational study reports an association but the headline says a food “prevents” disease, the headline has already outrun the design. A fast assessment is often possible from the paper’s title, abstract, methods summary and results table. A confident verdict requires more: the full methods, prespecified outcomes, limitations and wider evidence. Trace the claim back to the paper Scientific claims commonly pass through several layers: study → journal article → press release → news report → headline → social post At each stage, qualifications can disappear. A result limited to mice may become advice for people. A reduction in a biomarker may become prevention of disease. “Could help explain” may become “scientists prove”. Search the headline’s distinctive wording, the named institution, the lead author and the topic. A reputable report should identify the journal or link to the paper. If it does not, search Crossref or PubMed using the author and several title words.[3][4] Once found, confirm that it is the same study. Similar papers may examine different populations or outcomes. Note: the full title and authors; journal and publication date; article type; whether the paper is a preprint or peer reviewed publication; whether it has been corrected, withdrawn or retracted; whether the news story predates the final paper. A preprint is a publicly available manuscript that has not yet completed a journal’s peer review process. That does not make it false, but its status should remain visible in the headline and any summary. Read the press release sideways Compare the paper with the university, journal or company press release. Research by Petroc Sumner and colleagues found that exaggerated causal claims and human advice in health news were associated with similar exaggeration in academic press releases.[1] This means that “the university said it” is not enough; institutional publicity must be checked against the research. Look for wording shared verbatim across many news stories. Identical phrases may show that outlets relied on one press release rather than independently examining the paper. The number of articles repeating a claim is then a poor measure of evidential strength. Match the headline to the study design The design determines which conclusions are available. It does not decide whether every conclusion is correct, but it sets an upper limit on defensible language. Randomised trials In a randomised controlled trial, participants are assigned by chance to different interventions. Proper randomisation can balance known and unknown differences between groups, making causal inference more credible. Blinding, suitable controls, low dropout and adherence to the planned analysis also matter.[5] A trial does not automatically justify a broad headline. Check whether: allocation was genuinely random and concealed; the control group received placebo, usual care or another active treatment; participants and outcome assessors were blinded where possible; many participants dropped out; the reported outcome was prespecified; follow up was long enough; the sample resembled the people addressed by the headline. A small, short trial may support “the intervention changed this measure over eight weeks” without supporting “the intervention prevents long term disease”. Illustration 1 Headline to Paper Trail Observational studies Cohort, case control and cross sectional studies observe differences that already exist. They can identify associations and generate important evidence, especially where experiments would be impractical or unethical. But the exposed and unexposed groups may differ in ways that affect the result. If coffee drinkers have a different disease rate, possible explanations include coffee, smoking, income, occupation, sleep, diet, healthcare use or other characteristics. Statistical adjustment can reduce measured confounding, but it cannot guarantee that every relevant difference was measured correctly. Reverse causation is another risk: an apparent exposure may be a consequence rather than a cause. People in early stages of illness might change their diet, making the new diet appear associated with disease. A systematic review of causal language found that stronger inference often appeared in media and academic descriptions than study design alone warranted.[2] Unless additional causal methods and evidence justify it, observational findings should normally be described with terms such as “associated with” rather than “causes”, “prevents” or “protects”. Laboratory and animal studies Cell and animal experiments can reveal mechanisms and justify further research. They do not by themselves establish that an intervention is safe or effective in people. Doses, metabolism, disease models and experimental conditions may differ substantially from real world human use. A headline saying a compound “kills cancer” may refer to cancer cells exposed directly to a high concentration in a dish. The accurate claim concerns those cells under those conditions. It is not yet a clinical treatment claim. Check whether the headline preserves the experimental subject. “In mice”, “in cultured cells” and “in a computer model” are not disposable details. Surveys and experiments on behaviour A survey can estimate self reported beliefs or habits, but wording, sampling and non response affect its interpretation. An online experiment may establish that one message changed an immediate questionnaire response without showing that it changed real world behaviour. Watch for headlines that replace a measured proxy with a larger construct. A short test score is not automatically intelligence; an intention to exercise is not exercise; willingness to share a post is not verified sharing behaviour. How to Critically Appraise A Research Paper: A Simple 10 Minute Framework Channel: Dr Ollie Burton Open on YouTube Check who was studied Headlines often imply a wider population than the sample permits. A study of 80 university students can support conclusions about those participants under the tested conditions. It does not automatically describe children, older adults, other cultures or the national population. Look for: sample size; age and sex distribution; country and setting; recruitment method; inclusion and exclusion rules; baseline health or experience; the number who completed the study. A large sample reduces some forms of random uncertainty but does not repair a systematically unrepresentative sample. Ten thousand volunteers recruited from one specialist website may provide a precise estimate for that unusual group while poorly representing the public. Small samples can produce unstable estimates and exaggerated apparent effects, particularly when many outcomes or subgroups are examined.[6] The problem is not that every small study is worthless. It is that a dramatic estimate from a small sample usually deserves more uncertainty and replication than a headline conveys. Check whether the result comes from the full sample or a subgroup. “Treatment helps patients” may actually mean that no overall benefit was found,"},{"title":"The retracted vaccine paper","url":"/the-retracted-vaccine-paper/","description":"In 1998, The Lancet published a case series describing 12 children with developmental and intestinal symptoms.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"The retracted vaccine paper In 1998, The Lancet published a case series describing 12 children with developmental and intestinal symptoms. In 1998, The Lancet published a case series describing 12 children with developmental and intestinal symptoms. Its authors proposed further investigation of a possible connection with the combined measles, mumps and rubella vaccine, commonly called MMR. The retracted vaccine paper 1a7b83 Updated 2026 11 03 Introduction The present evidence is far stronger: large cohort studies, case control studies, meta analyses and systematic reviews have found no increased autism risk from MMR vaccination.[4][5][6] Yet the claim survived because retraction cannot erase a memorable causal story. Autism signs often become noticeable during the same period in which children receive routine vaccinations; alarming personal accounts are easier to remember than population statistics; and later institutional action was recast by some campaigners as suppression rather than correction. The case shows how a weak study can become a durable public myth before science completes its slower checks. What the 1998 paper actually reported The original paper, led by Andrew Wakefield, was titled “Ileal Lymphoid Nodular Hyperplasia, Non Specific Colitis, and Pervasive Developmental Disorder in Children”. It described 12 children referred to a paediatric gastroenterology unit with developmental disorders and gastrointestinal symptoms.[1] This was a case series: a descriptive report about selected patients. It was not a randomised trial, cohort study or case control comparison. There was no unvaccinated control group against which to compare autism rates, and the study was not designed to estimate whether autism occurred more frequently after MMR vaccination than without it. The paper relied substantially on parents’ recollections of when behavioural symptoms began relative to vaccination or illness. That creates a risk of recall error, especially when families are already searching for an explanation. Referral to a specialist unit also means the 12 children were not a representative sample of all vaccinated children or all autistic children. The paper’s own wording did not claim that it had proved an association. It said that it had not done so. Nevertheless, the suggested sequence—vaccination, intestinal symptoms, then developmental regression—provided a vivid narrative that could easily be reported as a causal finding. The public message exceeded the study At a press conference accompanying publication, Wakefield argued for suspending use of the combined MMR vaccine in favour of separate vaccines. That recommendation did not follow from the case series. The study had not compared combined and separate vaccines, established that either caused autism, or shown that separation would reduce risk. This mismatch was crucial. The cautious form—“a small uncontrolled case series raises a question”—was poorly suited to news headlines. The stronger message—“a common childhood vaccine may cause autism”—was simple, frightening and immediately relevant to parents facing a vaccination decision. The claim therefore entered public debate at a level of certainty the research could not support. Later arguments sometimes treated the paper as though it had been a failed large scale test of causation. It was not. It lacked the design needed to conduct that test in the first place. From publication to full retraction Concerns about the paper developed over years rather than in one decisive moment. This long chronology helped the claim survive because supporters could portray each new criticism as another contested episode rather than as the correction of a settled error. In 2004, The Lancet published a statement in which most of the original authors withdrew the interpretation that the paper had identified a possible causal link between MMR vaccination and autism.[3] The journal also acknowledged concerns about an undisclosed conflict of interest connected with litigation against vaccine manufacturers. Investigative reporting by Brian Deer subsequently examined the children’s medical histories, recruitment and funding behind the work. A 2011 BMJ investigation reported discrepancies between the published paper and medical records, including the timing and description of symptoms.[7] Another article examined commercial and legal interests surrounding proposed tests and products connected with the vaccine scare.[8] After proceedings before the UK General Medical Council, The Lancet fully retracted the paper in February 2010, stating that elements of it had been shown to be incorrect and contrary to the findings of the disciplinary panel.[2] Wakefield was later removed from the UK medical register. What the Walker Smith appeal did not decide John Walker Smith, one of the paper’s co authors, successfully appealed his erasure from the medical register in 2012. The High Court found serious problems in the disciplinary panel’s reasoning concerning his conduct and the distinction between clinical care and research.[9] This ruling is sometimes cited as though it restored the paper or vindicated the MMR autism hypothesis. It did neither. The appeal concerned the case against Walker Smith, not whether MMR vaccination causes autism. It did not reinstate the retracted article, reverse Wakefield’s professional sanction or replace the epidemiological studies testing the alleged association. Keeping these issues separate matters. A regulator’s procedural or evidential error in one doctor’s case would not itself establish a biological causal relationship. That question must be answered by studies capable of comparing outcomes in vaccinated and unvaccinated populations. Larger studies tested the claim properly Even before the full retraction, researchers were using population data to test whether autism diagnoses or developmental regression were associated with MMR vaccination. A 1999 study examined autism cases in a North Thames population and found no sudden increase in autism diagnoses after introduction of MMR, no clustering of developmental regression after vaccination and no difference in age at diagnosis between vaccinated and unvaccinated children.[10] A Danish cohort study published in 2002 included 537,303 children. It found no increased autism risk among children who received MMR vaccination and no association with age at vaccination, time since vaccination or vaccination date.[11] A UK case control study using a large primary care database likewise found no association between MMR vaccination and pervasive developmental disorders.[12] These designs did what the 1998 case series could not: they included comparison groups and enough children to estimate whether autism was unusually frequent after vaccination. An independent review by the US Institute of Medicine concluded in 2004 that the epidemiological evidence favoured rejection of a causal relationship between MMR vaccination and autism.[13] High risk families were examined directly Some parents remained concerned that an association might exist only in genetically susceptible children. Researchers therefore examined groups in which underlying autism likelihood was higher. A 2015 study of US children with older siblings found no harmful association between MMR vaccination and autism spectrum disorder, whether or not the older sibling had autism.[14] This did not support the idea that MMR triggered autism in children with a family predisposition. In 2019, another nationwide Danish cohort followed 657,461 children. It found that MMR vaccination did not increase autism risk, trigger autism in susceptible children or produce a clustering of cases during specified periods after vaccination.[4] Wakefield — One Retracted Vaccine Study Shook the World. Why Does Its Shadow Refuse to Fade? Channel: WowScience by TechSensia Open on YouTube Reviews combined the wider evidence A 2014 meta analysis covering cohort and case control studies, collectively involving more than 1.2 million children, found no association between vaccination and autism or autism spectrum disorder. It also found no association with MMR, thimerosal or mercury exposure from vaccines.[5] A Cochrane systematic review of vaccines against measles, mumps, rubella and chickenpox similarly found no credible evidence of an association between MMR and autism.[6] Cochrane reviewers also considered vaccine effectiveness and known adverse events, rather than pretending that vaccination has no risks whatsoever. The evidence is not merely that researchers failed to reproduce one small paper. Different teams, countries, databases and study designs repeatedly tested the proposed association at scales many thousands of times larger. Their results converged. Why timing felt like causation Autism is a neurodevelopmental condition. Signs may be present early, but differences in communication, social interaction or behaviour often become more noticeable during the"},{"title":"Verifying viral media","url":"/verifying-viral-media/","description":"To verify a viral photograph or video, do not begin by hunting for strange fingers, shadows or pixels - begin instead with the fact-checker's core tools,...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Verifying viral media To verify a viral photograph or video, do not begin by hunting for strange fingers, shadows or pixels begin instead with the fact checker's core tools,... To verify a viral photograph or video, do not begin by hunting for strange fingers, shadows or pixels begin instead with the fact checker's core tools, reverse image search and source tracing. Verifying viral media 2f8c59 Updated 2026 11 03 Introduction Treat metadata and artificial intelligence detectors as supporting clues, not verdicts. Metadata can be removed or altered, while detector performance can fall when media is compressed, cropped or produced by an unfamiliar generator.[3][4] The strongest conclusion usually comes from several independent signals pointing in the same direction. A file can be genuine but miscaptioned; an edited image can still document a real event; and authentic footage can be presented with a false explanation. Verification must therefore test both the media and the story attached to it. The fast verification workflow For an ordinary social media post, use this sequence before sharing: 1. Preserve the post. Record its link, account name, caption, upload time and any claimed location. 2. Define the claim. Write down who or what the media supposedly depicts, where it happened and when. 3. Find earlier copies. Reverse search the image or several video keyframes. 4. Trace the source. Look for the original photographer, filmer or first identifiable uploader. 5. Test place and time. Compare visible details with maps, street imagery, weather records, satellite images and other footage. 6. Inspect the file cautiously. Review edits, audio, metadata and provenance records without treating any single clue as decisive. 7. Seek independent confirmation. Find reporting, witnesses or records that do not all depend on the same upload. 8. State a bounded verdict. Use “verified”, “miscontextualised”, “altered”, “unsubstantiated” or “unresolved” rather than forcing every case into “real” or “fake”. The process is investigative rather than cosmetic. A convincing looking post can be false, while a blurred or heavily compressed recording can be authentic. The question is not whether the media “looks right”; it is whether its traceable history and external details support the attached claim. Preserve the claim before it changes Begin by capturing what is actually being alleged. Save the page address, take a screenshot showing the account and caption, and note the time displayed. If the claim could cause serious harm, preserve the original file where lawful and safe rather than relying only on a platform compressed copy. Record four elements separately: Identity: Who or what is supposedly shown? Location: Where was it allegedly recorded? Time: On what date, or during which event? Action: What does the post claim happened? This prevents a moving target. When an image is shown to be old, its promoter may retreat from “this happened today” to “this kind of thing happens”. The broader proposition may be arguable, but it does not validate the original caption. Do not assume that the account posting the media created it. Ask whether the uploader claims to be an eyewitness, credits another person or uses vague phrases such as “just received” and “reports say”. A social media timestamp proves only when that particular copy appeared, not when the scene was recorded. Preservation also matters because posts can be deleted, captions edited and usernames changed. The Internet Archive’s Wayback Machine can sometimes reveal earlier versions of ordinary web pages, although many social platforms restrict or frustrate archiving.[5] A missing archive is not evidence that a post never existed. Find the earliest available image Reverse image search compares an uploaded picture with images indexed elsewhere. Google Lens, TinEye and other engines use different indexes and matching systems, so trying more than one can uncover different results.[6][7] Use the highest quality version available. Then repeat the search with several variations: crop away captions, borders and reaction graphics; search the central scene rather than the whole screenshot; isolate a distinctive building, vehicle or person; rotate or unmirror the image if it may have been flipped; try both the complete frame and tight crops; search visible words separately as text. A successful match may reveal that a photograph labelled as today’s protest first appeared during an unrelated demonstration years earlier. It may also reveal an uncropped original whose missing edge changes the meaning of the scene. Search results are leads, not automatic proof. The oldest indexed result may be a repost, and search engines do not index every account, messaging service or deleted page. Establish the earliest version you can document, then examine whether it identifies a creator, agency, location or event. Illustration 1 The 8 Step Verification Workflow Follow the attribution chain If a news outlet credits a photographer or agency, search that name with a description of the scene. Photo agency captions commonly include the date, location and circumstances. Compare those details with the viral caption, but also check whether the agency record refers to the exact frame rather than a visually similar photograph. Where several sites use identical wording, they may all be copying one source. Independent publication does not necessarily mean independent verification. Follow links and credit lines backwards until the chain reaches the person who recorded the scene or an organisation that received the original file. Turn video into searchable images Reverse video search is less straightforward than reverse image search, so divide the recording into keyframes: still images representing distinct moments or camera views. The InVID WeVerify plugin includes tools for extracting keyframes and running image searches, while Amnesty International’s YouTube DataViewer has historically helped investigators locate upload information and thumbnails for YouTube videos.[8][9] Choose frames containing useful features: a landmark or skyline; a shopfront, road sign or number plate; a distinctive vehicle or uniform; an unusual stage, banner or room; the clearest view of a person; the moment before or after a suspected edit. Avoid relying on the opening frame alone. Recycled footage is often reframed with a new title card while the useful match appears later in the clip. Search multiple frames because one may be too blurred or generic to match. If results show a longer version, watch it from before the viral excerpt begins until after it ends. A short clip may conceal a staged setup, an explanation, an earlier provocation or the fact that a supposed explosion is part of a film production. Audio should be checked separately. Search distinctive spoken phrases, song lyrics or announcements. Compare the sound with the visible environment: echoes, traffic and crowd reactions can reveal discontinuities. Audio may also have been replaced without changing the pictures. Verification Challenge Chapter 1: Reverse Image Search (English AU/NZ) Channel: Google News Initiative Open on YouTube Test the claimed location Geolocation means identifying where media was recorded by matching visible features with external geographical information. Start with large, stable features before interpreting tiny details. Look for: street patterns and junction shapes; mountain profiles, coastlines and river bends; towers, bridges, stadiums and religious buildings; road markings, traffic direction and sign designs; public transport vehicles and route numbers; business names and telephone prefixes; utility poles, paving and street furniture; vegetation and terrain. Search maps and photographs for combinations rather than isolated features. A red bus or palm tree is rarely distinctive enough. A particular junction beside a curved glass building and a railway viaduct may be. OpenStreetMap can help identify roads, buildings and infrastructure, while Google Earth offers satellite imagery and, in some versions, historical views.[10][11] Mapillary and other street level imagery can assist where conventional street imagery is missing or outdated.[12] Compare the arrangement and viewing angle, not merely the presence of one similar object. A convincing geolocation should explain several features in the frame. Mark where the camera would have stood, its viewing direction and why the skyline or road layout fits. If one prominent landmark should be visible but is absent, investigate whether the camera angle, focal length, weather or construction date explains the discrepancy. Do not expose a private person’s precise location unnecessarily. Verification can establish a city, neighbourhood or public event without publishing a home address or other sensitive coordinates. Test the date and time Chronolocation—estimating when media was recorded—usually combines several imperfect clues. First,"},{"title":"When claims outlive their sources","url":"/when-claims-outlive-their-sources/","description":"A claim can outlive the information that told us how to judge it.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"When claims outlive their sources A claim can outlive the information that told us how to judge it. A claim can outlive the information that told us how to judge it. Memory may preserve the basic proposition—“product X causes illness”, for example—while losing the fact that it appeared in satire, came from an unreliable source or was explicitly corrected. When claims outlive their sources 7b5d34 Updated 2026 11 03 The Psychology of Memory: Every Major Way Memory Fails Explained Channel: Beyond Habit Mind Open on YouTube Introduction This is not inevitable, and source information does not always disappear before content. The risk rises when the claim is simple, its qualification is complicated, attention was divided, or much time has passed. It also rises when later encounters repeat the claim without restoring its original context. The crucial distinction is between remembering a statement and remembering its evidential status : who supplied it, what supported it and whether it was presented as true or false. Why the claim and its source come apart Memory does not store a statement, its source and every surrounding qualification as one inseparable record. According to the source monitoring framework , people retain features of an experience—its wording, meaning, emotional tone and context—and later infer where the memory came from.[3][4] That inference is usually efficient, but it can fail when contextual details are weak or similar sources become confused. Imagine reading, “A household ingredient prevents influenza”, in an article that immediately calls the claim unsupported. The ingredient and disease form a compact, memorable association. The reasons for rejection may require a longer chain: the cited experiment involved cells rather than patients, no clinical trial showed prevention, and the person promoting it lacked relevant expertise. If the association remains while that chain becomes inaccessible, the claim can feel known without being correctly evaluated. Experiments separating statement familiarity from source recollection illustrate the problem. Ian Begg and colleagues found that familiar statements could receive higher truth ratings when participants failed to recall the source that should have discredited them.[1] Other work indicates that readers can become more receptive to statements from low credibility sources as time separates the content from its origin.[5][6] This does not mean source memory always decays at a fixed, faster rate. Content and context vary greatly in memorability. A distinctive source may be remembered better than an ordinary claim, while a shocking attribution can become part of the claim itself. The narrower conclusion is that recognition can survive without successful source retrieval , leaving familiarity to influence judgement. Illustration 1 Memory Decay Chain Gist is efficient but incomplete People often remember the gist of information rather than its precise wording. Gist memory is useful: it allows someone to retain the point of a conversation without storing every sentence. But the gist may omit logical operators and qualifications such as “not”, “unconfirmed”, “associated with but not caused by”, or “true only under these conditions”. Research on learning from fictional stories provides a concrete example. Readers can absorb factual errors embedded in fiction and later reproduce them when answering general knowledge questions, even though the story was never presented as a factual reference source.[7][8] Remembering the proposition is not the same as remembering its fictional origin. A similar risk appears when headlines simplify research. The memorable residue may be “coffee prevents disease”, while the original article merely reported an observational association. Once the study design, sample and uncertainty have faded, later repetition can strengthen the shortened proposition rather than the more cautious finding. How warnings lose the race with familiarity Warnings can work immediately. A visible false label, correction or credibility cue gives the reader a reason to reject a claim. The difficulty is that the warning and the claim may have different memory profiles. In consumer research, Ian Skurnik and colleagues showed participants product claims accompanied by warnings that some were false. The warnings initially helped, but after a delay some participants—particularly older adults—misremembered warned against claims as true.[9] The appropriate conclusion is not that warnings are useless or inevitably counterproductive. It is that a warning must remain retrievable when the claim returns. Age can matter because source memory and associative binding often become less reliable in later adulthood, although individual differences are large and older people may compensate with knowledge and careful decision making.[10][11] Distraction, cognitive load and low attention can create similar problems at any age: the proposition receives enough processing to seem familiar, but its status receives too little to be retained. Memory reconstruction, source monitoring, and emotional memories Khan Academy Channel: khanacademymedicine Open on YouTube Corrections face the same separation problem Corrections often contain the original claim so readers know what is being addressed. This creates a structural challenge: the false proposition may be repeated in a short, vivid form, while its refutation is longer and less memorable. Later, the reader may recognise the proposition but fail to retrieve the correction. The continued influence effect is related but not identical. In studies of reports that were later retracted, people sometimes continued using the original information when reasoning about what happened.[12][13] A retraction may remove a detail without supplying another explanation capable of filling the gap. Source memory failure contributes when people remember the information but not that it was withdrawn; incomplete updating and preference for a coherent story can contribute as well.[14] The feared “familiarity backfire effect”—in which any repetition during correction supposedly makes misinformation more believable—has often been overstated. Experiments designed to detect it have generally found that clear corrections reduce false belief, even when they repeat the claim.[15] Meta analyses likewise find that debunking works overall, although effects vary with the original message, the correction and the time elapsed.[16][17] The practical problem is therefore not “never mention the myth”. It is ensuring that the corrective status remains bound to the claim. The sleeper effect has limits The classic sleeper effect offers a closely related idea: a persuasive message from a discredited source may gain influence over time if people retain the message but forget or detach the reason for discounting it. Early research compared identical communications attributed to high and low credibility sources and reported that the credibility gap could narrow after a delay.[18] Later research showed that this effect is conditional rather than automatic. A meta analysis found that delayed increases in persuasion were most likely when people noticed a strong discounting cue but did not permanently integrate it with the message.[19] If the source was never encoded, the message may be influential from the start; if the warning remains firmly connected to the claim, there is little reason for delayed persuasion. This distinction prevents overgeneralisation. A rumour does not become persuasive merely because it is old. What matters is the changing accessibility of different memory components: the content, the source, the warning and any alternative explanation. Illustration 2 Correction Strategies Keeping evidential status attached The most useful countermeasures follow directly from the mechanism. They make the source and correction easier to retrieve alongside the claim. Lead with the accurate account. Starting with the fact gives readers a replacement proposition rather than allowing the myth to become the organising idea. The false claim can then be identified once, clearly labelled and explained.[20] Explain the error rather than adding a bare label. “False” is a fragile tag. “This photograph was taken in another country five years earlier” supplies a concrete account that can later compete with the misleading caption. Corrections containing a coherent alternative tend to outperform simple retractions.[12][14] Bind the judgement to a reason. A warning such as “unreliable source” may be forgotten because it is generic. A specific explanation—fabricated quotation, retracted paper, altered denominator or non random sample—creates more retrieval routes. Repeat the correction, not only the controversy. Subsequent coverage should restate the accurate account and its basis. Otherwise each new discussion supplies another exposure to the claim while assuming that readers remember an earlier correction."},{"title":"Why repeated claims feel true","url":"/why-repeated-claims-feel-true/","description":"Repeated claims can begin to feel true even when repetition adds no evidence.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why repeated claims feel true Repeated claims can begin to feel true even when repetition adds no evidence. Repeated claims can begin to feel true even when repetition adds no evidence. Psychologists call this the illusory truth effect: encountering a statement before makes it easier to process, and that ease can be mistaken for accuracy. The effect is reliable but not magical. Why repeated claims feel true 4a9e68 Updated 2026 11 03 Introduction The risk is greatest when people recognise a claim without remembering where it came from, or when many repetitions appear to signal a social consensus. A rumour seen in a headline, video, search result and family message may therefore feel independently confirmed even if every version traces back to the same unsupported source. Understanding that mechanism helps explain why familiar falsehoods can survive disclaimers—and why slowing down to recover their origin matters. The Illusion of Truth Channel: Veritasium Open on YouTube Familiarity quietly becomes evidence The illusory truth effect has been studied since at least 1977, when Lynn Hasher, David Goldstein and Thomas Toppino asked participants to rate statements over several sessions. Repeated statements received higher truth ratings than comparable new ones, including when the repetition occurred weeks earlier.[3] A later meta analysis covering decades of experiments found a consistent truth effect across variations in timing, subject matter and procedure.[4] The basic experimental design is deliberately simple. Participants first read a mixture of statements, often without being asked to evaluate them. Later, they rate old and new statements for truth. Because repetition is controlled while the statements’ evidential support remains unchanged, any difference can be attributed to prior exposure rather than newly supplied facts. The effect is not confined to obscure trivia. Repetition can increase perceived truth even when a statement contradicts knowledge that participants could retrieve if prompted.[5][6] Nor does it require dozens of exposures. In experiments involving fabricated news headlines, a single previous encounter increased later accuracy ratings; the effect remained detectable after a week and occurred for politically concordant and discordant headlines.[7] That finding does not mean people accept every familiar headline. Average changes in rating can be modest, and many participants still reject the claim. The practical concern is cumulative: small shifts in confidence can affect whether someone pauses, investigates, shares or dismisses a warning. Why easy processing feels like truth The leading explanation centres on processing fluency —the subjective ease with which information enters and moves through the mind. Repetition increases fluency because the words, structure or underlying idea have already been processed. The next encounter therefore feels smoother. Fluency is not itself irrational evidence. Accurate statements are often repeated by teachers, institutions and other people, while coherent explanations tend to be easier to understand than incoherent ones. In ordinary life, familiarity can therefore correlate with reliability. The mistake arises when the mind uses that normally useful correlation in a situation where familiarity has been manufactured or is unrelated to truth. Experiments show that repetition is not the only route to this feeling. Statements made visually easier to read have sometimes received higher truth ratings than less fluent versions.[8] Rhyming aphorisms have also been judged more accurate than semantically equivalent non rhyming versions, apparently because their form makes them easier to process.[9] Even a non probative photograph—a picture that supplies no evidence for the relevant claim—can increase perceived truth by making the subject easier to imagine or bring to mind.[10] These effects are usually subtle and dependent on context. A polished layout, rhyme or photograph cannot make an obviously impossible proposition convincing to most readers. They matter most when a claim is plausible enough that people lack a decisive answer and must rely on indirect cues. Illustration 1 From Exposure to Belief Familiarity can be misattributed People experience fluency but do not always identify its source correctly. A sentence may feel familiar because it was seen in a correction, a satirical post, an unreliable advert or a list explicitly labelled false. At a later encounter, the feeling of recognition may remain while the reason for it has faded. This is sometimes described as familiarity misattribution . The mind registers “I have encountered this before” but may effectively translate that into “I think I know this” or “this has probably been established”. Research separating familiarity from source recollection shows that these processes can pull judgement in different directions: remembering that a statement came from an untrustworthy source can support rejection, while recognising the statement without retrieving its source can increase acceptance.[11][12] Fluency can also be reinterpreted. In one experiment, researchers reversed the usual truth effect by leading participants to associate familiarity with falsehood rather than truth.[13] This suggests that fluency is not a fixed truth detector. It is an ambiguous sensation whose meaning depends partly on what people believe caused it. Memory preserves claims better than labels A warning and the claim it qualifies are not necessarily stored or retrieved as a single durable package. The central proposition is often simple—“product X causes condition Y”—while the qualification is more complex: the study was retracted, the image is miscaptioned, the sample was biased or the relationship was correlational rather than causal. Over time, people may retain the proposition’s gist while losing its source and status. This is one reason warnings can disappoint. Consumer research has found that an immediate warning about false product claims can reduce belief, yet older adults in particular may later misremember the warned against claims as true as the warning’s context becomes less accessible.[14] The result should not be simplified into “warnings always backfire”; timing, age, attention and the warning’s design all matter. The same memory imbalance helps explain the continued influence effect , in which retracted information continues to shape reasoning. In classic experiments, participants read an unfolding account of a warehouse fire initially blamed on flammable materials. Even after being told those materials had not been present, they continued to use the initial explanation when answering questions about the fire.[15] A retraction was more effective when it supplied a coherent alternative explanation. Continued influence and illusory truth are related but distinct. Continued influence concerns how an initial explanation continues to structure reasoning after correction. Illusory truth concerns how prior exposure increases perceived accuracy. Both reveal the same practical vulnerability: memory for content can outlast memory for its evidential status.[16][17] Search results and social feeds can compound the problem. A misleading claim may appear first as an allegation, then in articles rejecting it, then in posts discussing the controversy. Later, a reader may remember that the subject and claim were repeatedly associated without remembering which sources endorsed or disproved the connection. Repetition has increased familiarity even when much of the coverage was corrective. Illusory Truth Effect — How Repetition Tricks Your Brain Into Believing Lies Channel: Bull and Thread Open on YouTube When repetition looks like consensus Repeated exposure can supply more than a feeling of cognitive ease. In social settings, it may also imply that many people independently hold the same view. Yet a single active account, syndicated article or copied message can generate numerous exposures. An experiment by Kimberly Weaver and colleagues found that hearing the same opinion repeatedly from one person could make it seem more widely shared, even when participants knew that the repetitions came from the same speaker.[18] Familiarity was being used as a rough cue to popularity. Online, where copied posts may arrive through several channels and attribution is easily lost, the distinction between “many encounters” and “many independent sources” becomes especially important. Platform metrics reinforce social cues. High view counts, visible shares and repeated recommendations can suggest that a claim has passed some collective test. In reality, popularity may reflect novelty, outrage, coordinated promotion or the platform’s recommendation system. A large scale study of Twitter found that false news in its dataset travelled farther and faster than true news, with novelty and human sharing contributing to the difference.[19] That result concerns diffusion, not"}]};
